    { id: "lolli2019", cite: "Lolli L, Batterham AM, Hawkins R, et al. Mathematical coupling causes spurious correlation within the conventional acute-to-chronic workload ratio calculations. Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(15):921-922. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2017-098110", grade: "methodological critique with simulation", found: "external", supports: "Because the latest week sits in both the acute and the chronic load, the two correlate (~0.5) from the arithmetic alone." },
    { id: "windt2019", cite: "Windt J, Gabbett TJ. Is it all for naught? What does mathematical coupling mean for acute:chronic workload ratios? Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(16):988-990. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2017-098925", grade: "methodological reply", found: "Zotero", supports: "Agrees coupling induces a spurious correlation; argues the practical consequences are limited. The 'researchers still argue' side." },
    { id: "gabbett2019", cite: "Gabbett TJ, Hulin B, Blanch P, Chapman P, Bailey D. To couple or not to couple? For acute:chronic workload ratios and injury risk, does it really matter? Int J Sports Med. 2019;40(9):597-600. doi:10.1055/a-0955-5589", grade: "single-cohort observational study", found: "Zotero", supports: "In 28 elite cricket fast bowlers, coupled and uncoupled ratios gave similar injury associations." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Open on the number. If you have been around load monitoring in the last ten years, you have heard "1.5". It comes from the acute:chronic workload ratio: this week's load divided by what the athlete has averaged over the last month or so.

The 2016 review that popularized it called 0.8 to 1.3 a "sweet spot" and 1.5 and above a "danger zone". That framing traveled fast, into apps, dashboards and staff meetings. Today is about what is worth keeping from it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.figure("1.5", { x: 0.7, y: 1.05, w: 5.6, h: 4.4, size: 280, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
        s.headline("The most quoted number in load monitoring.", { x: 6.9, y: 1.9, w: 5.7, h: 1.6, size: 40 });
        s.lead("It compares this week's load with what an athlete is used to. Above 1.5 became shorthand for danger.", { x: 6.9, y: 3.75, w: 5.5, h: 1.6, size: 22 });
        s.source("Gabbett, Br J Sports Med, 2016", { x: 6.9, y: 5.95, w: 5.5, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is the problem. What spread was the simple version: keep everyone between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.

What came after was messier. Reviews of the studies found the results inconsistent and often confusing. And the usual way of calculating the ratio counts this week in both the top and the bottom of the fraction, so the two move together whether or not anything real is going on. Researchers still argue about how much that matters in practice.

None of this means load does not matter. It means a single ratio is not a verdict on an athlete.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The number got simple. The science didn't.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.label("What spread", { x: 0.75, y: 2.75, w: 5.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.2, 5.3, 2.4, "Stay between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.", { font: "light", size: 30, lineH: 1.12 });
        s.line(6.62, 2.8, 6.62, 5.7, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What later work found", { x: 7.1, y: 2.75, w: 5.4 });
        s.text(7.1, 3.2, 5.45, 2.6, "Results are inconsistent, and this week sits on both sides of the ratio, so some of the link is arithmetic.", { font: "light", size: 25, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.source("Gabbett 2016 · Impellizzeri et al. 2020 · Lolli et al. 2019", { refs: ["gabbett2016", "impellizzeri2020", "lolli2019"] });
        s.cite("windt2019", "gabbett2019");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `What holds up is the idea underneath the number. Athletes tolerate hard work they have built up to. What gets them in trouble is a sudden jump well beyond what they are used to.

Read the scale left to right. 1.0 means this week looks like a normal week for that athlete. The shaded band is "close to usual". From 1.5 up is a spike: half again more than they are prepared for. In the rugby league data, players with a solid base handled ordinary weeks well, and their risk rose with spikes.

So the ratio is a way to spot spikes. That is its job, and that is where its job ends.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Sudden spikes are the risk, not hard work.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const X = r => 1.25 + (r - 0.5) * 7.0;
        const y = 4.6;
        s.rect(X(0.8), y - 0.62, X(1.3) - X(0.8), 0.78, { fill: "soft" });
        s.line(X(0.5), y, X(2.0) + 0.35, y, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
        s.line(X(1.5), y, X(2.0) + 0.35, y, { color: "accent", w: 7 });
        [0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.3, 1.5, 2.0].forEach(r => {
          const major = [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0].includes(r);
          const hot = r === 1.5;
          s.line(X(r), y - (major ? 0.14 : 0.09), X(r), y + (major ? 0.14 : 0.09), { color: hot ? "accent" : "muted", w: major ? 1.5 : 1 });
          s.text(X(r) - 0.6, y + 0.3, 1.2, 0.45, r.toFixed(1), { font: "cond", size: major ? 22 : 16, align: "center", color: hot ? "accent" : major ? "ink" : "muted" });
        });
        s.text(X(0.8), y - 1.2, 3.6, 0.45, "0.8–1.3: close to usual", { font: "cond", size: 20, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(1.5), y - 1.2, 3.8, 0.45, "1.5 and up: a spike", { font: "condBold", size: 20, color: "accent" });
        s.text(X(0.5), y + 0.95, 4, 0.4, "Less than usual", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(2.0) + 0.35 - 4, y + 0.95, 4, 0.4, "More than usual", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Lines as drawn in Gabbett 2016; spike finding from Hulin et al. 2016", { refs: ["gabbett2016", "hulin2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So how do we use it here? As a question, not a red light. Three steps, in order.

First, know where the jumps live: the first days of camp, the return from an injury, the week back from a break. Those are predictable.

Second, when the number jumps, ask why. Was it planned, or did it creep up through extra sessions nobody added up?

Third, any change to a plan goes through the people who own it: the sport coach, and Athletic Medicine for anyone in rehab or return to play. The number starts the conversation. It never ends it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Use it as a question, not a red light.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const cols = [
          ["1", "Know where jumps live: camp, breaks, returns from injury."],
          ["2", "When it jumps, ask: planned, or crept up?"],
          ["3", "Bring in the coach and Athletic Medicine."],
        ];
        cols.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const x = 0.75 + i * 4.0;
          s.line(x, 2.95, x + 3.55, 2.95, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(x, 3.15, 1.2, 0.8, k, { font: "cond", size: 40, color: "muted", lineH: 0.9 });
          s.text(x, 4.0, 3.55, 2.0, t, { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.18 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.15, 12.58, 6.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("hulin2016");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `One line to take away: ramp, don't spike.

The big jumps are on the calendar before they happen: the first week of camp, the week back from a break. If we plan those weeks on purpose, we get the benefit of hard work without the jump.

Question for the room: where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it? Give people a moment; the answers are usually specific and useful.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Ramp, don't spike.", { y: 1.35, w: 11.5, h: 1.8, size: 104 });
        s.lead("Spikes show up on the calendar first. Plan those weeks on purpose.", { y: 3.45, w: 9.2, h: 1.3, size: 24 });
        s.line(0.75, 5.0, 1.75, 5.0, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.2, 11.8, 1.0, "Where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it?", { font: "medium", size: 26, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

