    { id: "soligard2016", cite: "Soligard T, Schwellnus M, Alonso JM, et al. How much is too much? (Part 1) International Olympic Committee consensus statement on load in sport and risk of injury. Br J Sports Med. 2016;50(17):1030-1041. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2016-096581", grade: "consensus statement", found: "external (cited across Zotero items)", supports: "Defines load broadly: training and competition load, calendar congestion, psychological load and travel." },
    { id: "hamlin2019", cite: "Hamlin MJ, Wilkes D, Elliot CA, Lizamore CA, Kathiravel Y. Monitoring training loads and perceived stress in young elite university athletes. Front Physiol. 2019;10:34. doi:10.3389/fphys.2019.00034", grade: "four-year observational cohort (n = 182)", found: "external", supports: "Energy and sleep deteriorated toward exam periods; exam periods coincided with the highest stress and more illness; mood, sleep and academic stress were associated with injury." },
    { id: "ivarsson2017", cite: "Ivarsson A, Johnson U, Andersen MB, Tranaeus U, Stenling A, Lindwall M. Psychosocial factors and sport injuries: meta-analyses for prediction and prevention. Sports Med. 2017;47(2):353-365. doi:10.1007/s40279-016-0578-x", grade: "meta-analysis (48 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Stress responses and a history of stressors had the strongest associations with injury rates among the psychosocial factors studied." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 50,
      notes: `Here is a finding from a Division I football program. Researchers tracked about a hundred players across a season and sorted the weeks into three kinds: training camp, ordinary in-season weeks, and weeks with midterms and finals.

Camp was the riskiest stretch, as you would expect. But exam weeks came next. The odds of a player being on an injury restriction were about 1.8 times higher in exam weeks than in ordinary weeks. Nothing about practice changed. The calendar did.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Exams showed up in the injury report.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.3, w: 10.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.figure("1.78×", { x: 6.4, y: 3.05, w: 6.18, h: 2.0, size: 150, align: "right", refs: ["mann2016"] });
        s.label("Odds of an injury restriction, exam weeks vs. ordinary weeks", { x: 5.4, y: 5.2, w: 7.18, h: 0.36, size: 14, align: "right" });
        s.source("Mann et al., J Strength Cond Res, 2016 · one Division I football team, n = 101", { x: 4.6, y: 5.95, w: 7.98, align: "right", refs: ["mann2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The problem is that each of us sees one slice. Athletic Performance sees the session. Academics sees the exam schedule. Our mental health staff see the stress. Each slice can look fine on its own.

The IOC's consensus statement on load defines it broadly on purpose: training and games, but also travel, a crowded calendar and psychological stress. A four-year study of university athletes found the same pattern we see here. Energy and sleep slipped as exams got closer, and exam periods brought the highest stress and more illness.

The athlete doesn't experience slices. They carry the total.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.75, 0, 4.583, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("Each unit sees one slice of the load.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.25, w: 7.4, h: 1.5, size: 44 });
        const rows = ["Athletic Performance sees the session.", "Academics sees the exam schedule.", "Mental health sees the stress."];
        rows.forEach((t, i) => s.text(0.75, 3.15 + i * 0.85, 7.6, 0.6, t, { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.1 }));
        s.label("What the IOC counts as load", { x: 9.3, y: 2.3, w: 3.3 });
        s.text(9.3, 2.75, 3.3, 2.4, "Training, games, travel and life stress.", { font: "medium", size: 28, lineH: 1.12 });
        s.source("Soligard et al., Br J Sports Med, 2016", { x: 9.3, y: 5.95, w: 3.3, refs: ["soligard2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `Stack the slices and you get the picture on this slide. This is an illustrative shape, not anyone's data.

The bottom band is training. In this semester it stays flat. Someone looking only at practice would say nothing changed. The middle band is academic load, which climbs at midterms and again at finals. The top band is everything else in a life: relationships, money, home.

The gold line is the total, and it spikes twice while practice never moved. That matches the football finding and the four-year university study. A meta-analysis of psychosocial factors points the same way: stress responses had the strongest link to injury of anything studied.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Total load can spike while practice stays flat.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 8.2, h: 2.0, size: 56 });
        const x0 = 0.75, x1 = 10.6, yb = 6.0, W0 = 1, W1 = 16;
        const X = w => x0 + (w - W0) / (W1 - W0) * (x1 - x0);
        const ws = []; for (let w = W0; w <= W1 + 1e-6; w += 0.25) ws.push(w);
        const t1 = w => training(w), t2 = w => t1(w) + academics(w), t3 = w => t2(w) + life(w);
        const band = (lo, hi, fill, opacity) => {
          const top = ws.map(w => [X(w), yb - hi(w)]);
          const bot = ws.slice().reverse().map(w => [X(w), yb - lo(w)]);
          s.path([...top, ...bot], { fill, opacity, color: fill, w: 0.01 });
        };
        band(() => 0, t1, "rule", 1);
        band(t1, t2, "muted", 0.42);
        band(t2, t3, "muted", 0.2);
        s.path(ws.map(w => [X(w), yb - t3(w)]), { color: "accent", w: 4 });
        s.line(x0, yb, x1, yb, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        const lab = (y, t, font, color) => s.text(x1 + 0.15, y - 0.2, 1.85, 0.4, t, { font, size: 18, color, valign: "middle" });
        lab(yb - t3(W1) - 0.05, "Total", "condBold", "accent");
        lab(yb - (t2(W1) + t3(W1)) / 2 + 0.12, "Life", "cond", "ink");
        lab(yb - (t1(W1) + t2(W1)) / 2 + 0.05, "Academics", "cond", "ink");
        lab(yb - t1(W1) / 2, "Training", "cond", "ink");
        s.text(X(8) - 1, yb - t3(8) - 0.45, 2, 0.34, "Midterms", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.text(X(15.2) - 1, yb - t3(15.2) - 0.45, 2, 0.34, "Finals", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.source("Pattern: Mann et al. 2016 · Hamlin et al. 2019 · Ivarsson et al. 2017", { x: 0.75, y: 6.2, w: 7.4, refs: ["mann2016", "hamlin2019", "ivarsson2017"] });
        s.text(8.4, 6.2, 4.18, 0.32, "Illustrative shape, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right", valign: "bottom" });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So what do we do with it? Two moves, both cheap.

When exam weeks are coming, and they are on the academic calendar months ahead, put them on the training calendar too. The football authors suggest the obvious one: don't stack max testing or conditioning tests on top of finals.

When an athlete's sleep or mood drops in our check-ins, ask about school and life before adding or cutting work. Sometimes the answer is in the training plan. Often it's a paper due Thursday.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Plan around the whole calendar.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.label("When", { x: 0.75, y: 2.55, w: 3 });
        s.label("Do", { x: 5.6, y: 2.55, w: 3 });
        const rows = [
          ["Exam weeks are coming", "Put them on the training calendar. No max testing that week."],
          ["Sleep or mood drops", "Ask about school and life before changing the work."],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([w, d], i) => {
          const y = 3.0 + i * 1.55;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 4.5, 1.1, w, { font: "condBold", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
          s.text(5.6, y + 0.22, 6.98, 1.1, d, { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.15 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.1, 12.58, 6.1, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("mann2016");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: count all of it. Training load is the part we measure best, which is exactly why it is easy to mistake it for the whole thing.

Question for the room: which exam weeks are on our training calendar this semester? If nobody knows, that is the first fix, and it costs nothing.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Count all of it.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.3, w: 8, h: 1.5, size: 80 });
        s.lead("Practice is the part we measure best. It is not the whole load.", { x: 0.75, y: 2.85, w: 6.4, h: 1.2, size: 22 });
        s.text(6.2, 4.3, 6.38, 1.9, "Which exam weeks are on our training calendar this semester?", { font: "medium", size: 32, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

