// 13 · The Transition Buffer: the hour after practice is for recovery, not for new material.
// Composition: hook = assumed vs. measured in two plain columns; takeaway = the question is the
// whole slide; alignment = left. Insight hero bleeds off the left edge.

module.exports = {
  num: 13,
  slug: "the-transition-buffer",
  title: "The Transition Buffer",
  category: "Cross-Functional Integration",
  palette: "forest",
  hero: { layout: "timeline-gap", concept: "Practice block bleeding in from the left, a 60-90 minute gap for food, fluid and a shower, then class: the gap is the subject, labeled as a planning rule rather than a tested number" },
  compose: { hook: "assume-vs-measured", takeaway: "question-only", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Exercise doesn't fry the brain. Thirst, hunger and a rushed hour do, so build the buffer into the schedule.",
  question: "What do we schedule in the first hour after practice that we expect athletes to remember?",
  caveats: `Be straight about the evidence. Most studies of exercise and thinking use short lab bouts, often cycling, and measure within minutes; they find small improvements after exercise (Chang et al. 2025; Lambourne & Tomporowski 2010). They are not studies of two-hour practices in heat, so don't claim practice sharpens athletes for class either. The dehydration finding is a meta-analysis of lab studies: impairment was small overall and larger above 2% body-mass loss, mainly in attention, executive function and motor coordination. The 60-90 minute buffer has no study behind it. It comes from the EAI proposal and rests on reasoning: time to eat, drink, shower and settle. Say "planning rule" every time. Note for Eric: deck 01 (Post-Practice Cognitive Deficit) frames the hour after practice as a cognitive dip; this deck says the acute-exercise literature mostly shows the opposite and puts the drag on fluid, food and the rush. Reconcile the two before both are presented.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "chang2025", cite: "Chang YK, Ren FF, Li RH, Ai JY, Kao SC, Etnier JL. Effects of acute exercise on cognitive function: a meta-review of 30 systematic reviews with meta-analyses. Psychol Bull. 2025;151(2):240-259. doi:10.1037/bul0000460", grade: "meta-review (30 meta-analyses, 383 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Acute exercise improved cognitive function with a small-to-medium effect; benefits were seen when cognition was assessed after exercise." },
    { id: "lambourne2010", cite: "Lambourne K, Tomporowski P. The effect of exercise-induced arousal on cognitive task performance: a meta-regression analysis. Brain Res. 2010;1341:12-24. doi:10.1016/j.brainres.2010.03.091", grade: "meta-regression", found: "external", supports: "Cognitive performance was slightly impaired in the first 20 minutes during exercise and improved after exercise (mean effect 0.20), including after fatiguing exercise." },
    { id: "wittbrodt2018", cite: "Wittbrodt MT, Millard-Stafford M. Dehydration impairs cognitive performance: a meta-analysis. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2018;50(11):2360-2368. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000001682", grade: "meta-analysis (33 studies)", found: "external (cited in Zotero item Skotniczny et al. 2026)", supports: "Dehydration impaired attention, executive function and motor coordination, with greater impairment above 2% body-mass loss." },
    { id: "acsm2007", cite: "American College of Sports Medicine; Sawka MN, Burke LM, Eichner ER, Maughan RJ, Montain SJ, Stachenfeld NS. American College of Sports Medicine position stand. Exercise and fluid replacement. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2007;39(2):377-390. doi:10.1249/mss.0b013e31802ca597", grade: "position stand", found: "external", supports: "Defines excessive dehydration as more than 2% body-weight loss; after exercise, the goal is to replace any fluid and electrolyte deficit." },
    { id: "eai", cite: "Baylor Athletics Applied Performance. Educational Alignment Initiative: a strategic coordination framework (internal proposal). Education_Alignment_Initiative repository, docs/program/Educational_Alignment_Initiative_Proposal.md.", grade: "internal planning document; reasoning, not a study", found: "internal", supports: "Proposes a 60-90 minute buffer after practice for a meal, a shower and decompression before demanding academic work." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 50,
      notes: `Most of us carry an assumption about the hour after practice: they're cooked, nothing will stick. It's why we feel guilty scheduling a study session at 5:30.

Here's what the research on exercise and thinking actually shows. Across thirty meta-analyses, thinking is usually a little sharper after a bout of exercise, not duller. The effect is small, and those are mostly short lab sessions, not a two-hour practice in August. But it does mean exercise itself is not the villain. So what is?`,
      build(s) {
        s.label("What we assume", { x: 0.75, y: 1.5, w: 5.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.0, 5.3, 2.6, "Hard practice leaves the brain too tired to learn.", { font: "light", size: 36, lineH: 1.1, color: "muted" });
        s.line(6.62, 1.55, 6.62, 5.4, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What the research shows", { x: 7.1, y: 1.5, w: 5.4 });
        s.text(7.1, 2.0, 5.45, 2.6, "Thinking is usually a little sharper after exercise, not duller.", { font: "medium", size: 36, lineH: 1.1, color: "accent" });
        s.source("Chang et al., Psychol Bull, 2025 · Lambourne & Tomporowski, Brain Res, 2010", { x: 7.1, y: 5.95, w: 5.48, refs: ["chang2025", "lambourne2010"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The drag after practice is more ordinary: thirst, hunger and the clock.

A meta-analysis of thirty-three studies found that dehydration dulls attention, executive function and coordination, and the effect gets bigger once athletes lose more than 2% of their body mass in sweat. That's well within reach of a hot practice. The ACSM's position is that after exercise the job is to replace that fluid.

Now picture the usual sequence: practice ends at 5:15, the session starts at 5:30, and the athlete walks in thirsty, unfed and still in practice gear. That's the problem we can fix.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The drag after practice is thirst, hunger and the rush.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 8.4, h: 2.0, size: 56 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.6, 8.2, 1.6, "Losing more than 2% of body mass in sweat dulls attention and decision-making.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.18 });
        s.source("Wittbrodt & Millard-Stafford, Med Sci Sports Exerc, 2018 · ACSM position stand, 2007", { x: 0.75, y: 5.95, w: 9, refs: ["wittbrodt2018", "acsm2007"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So the buffer is the point. Practice on the left, class or the study session on the right, and between them a planned gap: eat something real, drink, shower, decompress with teammates.

Our EAI proposal sets that gap at 60 to 90 minutes. I want to be plain about where that number comes from. It's a planning rule built from what has to happen in that hour, not a figure from a study. Nobody has run the trial of 45 minutes against 90.

What the research does support is the content of the gap: replace the fluid, refuel, and stop treating the minutes after practice as free teaching time.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The gap is part of the schedule.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 9, h: 0.85, size: 40 });
        const y = 3.0, h = 1.35;
        s.rect(0, y, 4.1, h, { fill: "soft" });
        s.text(0.75, y + 0.42, 3.2, 0.5, "Practice", { font: "cond", size: 24, color: "ink" });
        s.rect(9.15, y, 3.43, h, { fill: "soft" });
        s.text(9.45, y + 0.42, 3.0, 0.5, "Class or study", { font: "cond", size: 24, color: "ink" });
        s.line(4.35, y + h / 2, 8.9, y + h / 2, { color: "accent", w: 2, arrow: "both" });
        s.text(4.35, y - 0.95, 4.55, 0.85, "60–90 minutes", { font: "condBold", size: 44, color: "accent", align: "center", lineH: 1.0, valign: "bottom" });
        s.text(4.35, y + h + 0.2, 4.55, 0.9, "Eat, drink, shower, decompress", { font: "light", size: 22, align: "center", lineH: 1.15 });
        s.line(4.35, y - 0.05, 4.35, y + h + 0.05, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        s.line(8.9, y - 0.05, 8.9, y + h + 0.05, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.55, 9, 0.4, "A planning rule from our EAI proposal, not a tested number.", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Why the gap: Wittbrodt & Millard-Stafford 2018 · ACSM 2007", { x: 0.75, y: 6.2, w: 9, refs: ["eai", "wittbrodt2018", "acsm2007"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `Here's the Monday version. Put a meal and about an hour between practice and anything you want remembered: tutoring, a new playbook install, a nutrition talk, a degree-audit meeting.

If something truly has to happen right after practice, keep it to logistics. Bus times, a handout in the locker, a two-minute reminder. Things that don't need to be learned, just heard.

This needs no budget. It needs the units that schedule athlete time to look at the same calendar, which is the subject of the coordination deck.`,
      build(s) {
        s.line(0.75, 2.0, 12.58, 2.0, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.35, 11.5, 2.0, "Put a meal and an hour between practice and anything you want remembered.", { font: "medium", size: 40, lineH: 1.1 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.65, 10.5, 1.0, "If it has to happen right after practice, keep it to logistics: times, buses, handouts.", { font: "light", size: 22, color: "muted", lineH: 1.2 });
