// 14 · Coordination Over Control: alignment means units talk to each other, not through one office.
// Composition: hook = a two-line quote set right with its source under it; takeaway = split
// columns (question | line) with a vertical rule; alignment = right rail throughout.

module.exports = {
  num: 14,
  slug: "coordination-over-control",
  title: "Coordination Over Control",
  category: "Cross-Functional Integration",
  palette: "mint",
  hero: { layout: "hub-vs-mesh", concept: "Two small networks of the same five units: control routes everything through one hub; coordination connects the units to each other, with a connector in the middle that keeps the calendar" },
  compose: { hook: "quote-right", takeaway: "split-columns", align: "right" },
  oneIdea: "Alignment isn't one office approving everything. It's units that know each other's plans and talk directly.",
  question: "Where does a decision wait on one office when two units could just talk?",
  caveats: `Ekstrand et al. is elite European soccer: 36 clubs, medical staff rating their own club's communication. It is a correlation, not proof that better communication prevents injuries, and the ratings come from one side of the relationship. Gittell et al. is hospital surgery, not athletics; it is here for the idea of relational coordination (frequent, timely, accurate communication plus shared goals, shared knowledge and mutual respect), which improved quality of care and reduced postoperative pain. Rimer et al. is a practitioner article from a Division I department, not a study. Tenan and Alejo is an editorial. The network drawing on slide 3 is a diagram of two ways to organize, not data. The EAI coordinator role is described in our own proposal as a connector, not a controller; this deck is the plain-language version of that idea.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "rimer2024", cite: "Rimer E, Petway A, Jones P, et al. Building comprehensive integration of health and performance support through sport science. Strength Cond J. 2024;46(1):55-68. doi:10.1519/SSC.0000000000000794", grade: "practitioner article", found: "Zotero", supports: "'A successful sport science department would not try to own every service it introduces. Rather, it would empower those from other disciplines to perform the services.' Separate unit directors with different philosophies can preserve silos." },
    { id: "ekstrand2019", cite: "Ekstrand J, Lundqvist D, Davison M, D'Hooghe M, Pensgaard AM. Communication quality between the medical team and the head coach/manager is associated with injury burden and player availability in elite football clubs. Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(5):304-308. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2018-099411", grade: "multi-club observational study (36 clubs)", found: "external (cited in Zotero items Rimer et al. 2024; Taberner et al. 2020)", supports: "Clubs with low-quality communication between head coach and medical team had higher injury burden and more severe injuries, lower training attendance (76% vs 83%) and lower match availability (82% vs 88%)." },
    { id: "gittell2000", cite: "Gittell JH, Fairfield KM, Bierbaum B, et al. Impact of relational coordination on quality of care, postoperative pain and functioning, and length of stay: a nine-hospital study of surgical patients. Med Care. 2000;38(8):807-819. doi:10.1097/00005650-200008000-00005", grade: "multi-site cross-sectional study (9 hospitals, 878 patients)", found: "external", supports: "Relational coordination (frequent, timely, accurate communication, problem-solving, shared goals, shared knowledge, mutual respect) improved quality of care and reduced postoperative pain." },
    { id: "tenan2024", cite: "Tenan MS, Alejo B. Athlete health and human performance will not improve without transdisciplinary collaboration and data sharing in elite sport. J Athl Train. 2024;59(10):979-983. doi:10.4085/1062-6050-0580.23", grade: "editorial (expert opinion)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Argues the 'performance director' should be an information hub that helps disciplines work together, not an administrative layer." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 50,
      notes: `This line comes from a Division I sport science group at Louisville, writing about how to build integrated health and performance support. "A successful sport science department would not try to own every service it introduces." They go on: it would empower people in other disciplines to do the work.

That's an unusual thing for a department to say about itself, and it's the posture I want for our coordination work. Today is about what alignment actually looks like when nobody is trying to be in charge of everything.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(3.6, 1.7, 8.98, 2.6, "“A successful sport science department would not try to own every service it introduces.”", { font: "cond", size: 46, color: "accent", align: "right", lineH: 1.08 });
        s.source("Rimer et al., Strength Cond J, 2024", { x: 6.58, y: 4.55, w: 6.0, h: 0.4, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top", refs: ["rimer2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Why does this matter? Because the cost of units not talking shows up in the athletes.

