module.exports = {
  num: 15,
  slug: "core-themes-that-cross-every-unit",
  title: "Core Themes That Cross Every Unit",
  category: "Cross-Functional Integration",
  palette: "sand",
  hero: { layout: "unit-theme-dot-matrix", concept: "Five units down the side, topics across: sleep, stress and time are filled for every unit, while each unit's specialty is filled only once. An illustrative map, not a survey" },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "headline-rule-question", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Sleep, stress and time touch every unit. Agree on one plain message for each and let every unit repeat it.",
  question: "Which theme would we align on first: sleep, stress or time?",
  caveats: `The dot matrix on slide 3 is an illustrative map of where these topics come up, built from the unit list in our EAI proposal; it is not a survey of what each unit taught. The 42% figure is one university (Stanford, 628 athletes across 29 teams), self-report, using a modified sleep questionnaire; don't present it as a national rate. Hamlin et al. followed 182 university athletes in New Zealand for four years; their odds ratios are per unit change in wellness scores, so keep the finding qualitative. "Time" has no number on the slides on purpose: the NCAA's time-demand survey figures couldn't be verified against the primary report in time, so they were cut. Stress is a shared theme for education; anything clinical goes to Baylor's licensed mental health staff, and this deck is not training in how to respond to a struggling athlete.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "mah2018", cite: "Mah CD, Kezirian EJ, Marcello BM, Dement WC. Poor sleep quality and insufficient sleep of a collegiate student-athlete population. Sleep Health. 2018;4(3):251-257. doi:10.1016/j.sleh.2018.02.005", grade: "single-university cross-sectional survey (n = 628)", found: "external (cited in Zotero items, e.g. Lever 2024; De Souza et al. 2024)", supports: "42.4% of athletes reported poor sleep quality (PSQI > 5); 39.1% regularly slept under 7 hours on weekdays." },
    { id: "hamlin2019", cite: "Hamlin MJ, Wilkes D, Elliot CA, Lizamore CA, Kathiravel Y. Monitoring training loads and perceived stress in young elite university athletes. Front Physiol. 2019;10:34. doi:10.3389/fphys.2019.00034", grade: "four-year observational cohort (n = 182)", found: "external", supports: "Energy and sleep deteriorated toward exams; exam periods coincided with the highest stress and more illness." },
    { id: "eai", cite: "Baylor Athletics Applied Performance. Educational Alignment Initiative: a strategic coordination framework (internal proposal). Education_Alignment_Initiative repository, docs/program/Educational_Alignment_Initiative_Proposal.md.", grade: "internal planning document", found: "internal", supports: "Names sleep, stress and time management as core themes where units can align, and describes three separate sessions launched by three units for one problem (freshman transition stress)." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Quick show of hands. How many of our units talked to athletes about sleep this year?

Let the hands go up. Usually it's most of the room: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, nutrition, mental health, academics, often coaches too.

Now the follow-up, which is the real point of today: did we all tell them the same thing?`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline(["How many of our units talked to athletes about ", { t: "sleep", color: "accent" }, " this year?"], { x: 0.75, y: 1.45, w: 10.6, h: 3.6, size: 80 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `These three topics aren't anyone's private territory.

Sleep: in one large university sample, 42% of athletes reported poor sleep quality, and nearly four in ten slept under seven hours on weeknights. Stress: a four-year study of university athletes found energy and sleep sliding toward exams, with exam periods bringing the highest stress and more illness. Time: full-time study on top of a sport, every week.

Our own EAI proposal describes what happens next. Three units see the same problem, freshman transition stress, and launch three separate sessions: time management, stress reduction, a training orientation. Three sessions, one topic, and athletes hear three versions.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Sleep, stress and time touch every unit.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Sleep", "42% of athletes in one large university sample slept poorly.", "Mah et al. 2018"],
          ["Stress", "Stress and illness peaked at exam time over four years.", "Hamlin et al. 2019"],
          ["Time", "Full-time study on top of a sport, every week.", ""],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([k, t, src], i) => {
          const y = 2.55 + i * 1.2;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 2.2, 0.6, k, { font: "condBold", size: 28, lineH: 1.0 });
          s.text(3.0, y + 0.24, 7.2, 0.9, t, { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.12 });
          if (src) s.source(src, { x: 10.3, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.28, h: 0.42, size: 13, align: "right", valign: "top" });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.15, 12.58, 6.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("mah2018", "hamlin2019");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `Here's the map. Five units down the side. Across the top, the three shared themes, then each unit's own specialty.

